From 2769cf221330d600af575de12c86091ff02043d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 21:49:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] bfd: microblaze: keep the PLT address of address-taken
 functions in executables

When a non-PIC executable takes the address of a function defined in
a shared library, the linker gives the executable a PLT entry and
must leave that PLT address as the value of the executable's
undefined symbol so that the dynamic linker treats it as the
canonical address: every reference in the process, including the
library's own and dlsym, then resolves to it and function pointers
compare equal as C requires.  The backend already records
pointer_equality_needed in check_relocs and points the symbol at the
PLT in allocate_dynrelocs, but finish_dynamic_symbol then zeroes the
value unconditionally.

On MicroBlaze &strlen in an executable is its PLT stub while
dlsym (RTLD_DEFAULT, "strlen") and every function pointer libc hands
out is the real function, so callbacks and dladdr on function
pointers misbehave: glibc's misc/tst-ldbl-errorfptr and elf/tst-addr1
fail.

Keep the value unless the symbol is only weakly referenced or never
had a pointer-equality relocation, as elf32-arm does.

bfd/ChangeLog:
	* elf32-microblaze.c (microblaze_elf_finish_dynamic_symbol): Only
	zero the value of a PLT-resolved undefined symbol when it is
	weak or pointer equality does not matter.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 bfd/elf32-microblaze.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bfd/elf32-microblaze.c b/bfd/elf32-microblaze.c
index 86429e772a4..0b0c4bc288b 100644
--- a/bfd/elf32-microblaze.c
+++ b/bfd/elf32-microblaze.c
@@ -3333,9 +3333,17 @@ microblaze_elf_finish_dynamic_symbol (struct bfd_link_info *info,
       if (!h->def_regular)
 	{
 	  /* Mark the symbol as undefined, rather than as defined in
-	     the .plt section.  Zero the value.  */
+	     the .plt section.  If the symbol is weak we need to clear
+	     the value.  Otherwise, the PLT entry would provide a
+	     definition for the symbol even if the symbol wasn't defined
+	     anywhere, and so the symbol would never be NULL.  Leave the
+	     value if there were any relocations where pointer equality
+	     matters (this is a clue for the dynamic linker, to make
+	     function pointer comparisons work between an application
+	     and shared library).  */
 	  sym->st_shndx = SHN_UNDEF;
-	  sym->st_value = 0;
+	  if (!h->ref_regular_nonweak || !h->pointer_equality_needed)
+	    sym->st_value = 0;
 	}
     }
 
